# Feature Spec — Doctrine Missions

**Author:** Architect
**Date:** 2026-09-15
**Recon map:** `doctrine-missions-recon-map`
**Upstream PRD:** `doctrine-missions-prd`

---

## Part 1 — Intent and Requirements

### Summary

A doctrine stops being a pair of files with a satellite of Knight personas and
becomes one directory: `<doctrine>.design.md` for the orchestrator, and one
`missions/<id>.md` of prose for each worker. Each mission file carries what used
to be split between a YAML step's `notes:` and a Knight's persona body. Lore
parses no workflow structure out of a doctrine any more — no step ids, no
`type`, no `phase`, no `needs` — and the Knight entity is removed outright:
module, CLI group, public API names, schema, health scope and seeded files.

A Lore mission points at a doctrine mission by the reference
`<doctrine-id>/<mission-id>`, stored verbatim and resolved only at read time, so
`lore show <mission-id>` hands a worker its feature-specific description and its
reusable instructions in one call. There is exactly one authoritative copy of
every reusable instruction and it lives beside the doctrine that uses it.

### Success Criteria

Every criterion below is checkable by running the command named.

- `lore doctrine show <id>` prints the design document and a `--- Missions ---`
  index in one call, and exits 0.
- `lore doctrine show <id> --mission <mission-id>` prints exactly that mission
  file's body, frontmatter stripped, and exits 0.
- `lore doctrine show <id> --mission nope` exits 1 with
  `Mission "nope" not found in doctrine "<id>"`, distinct from the message a
  missing doctrine produces.
- `lore doctrine new <name> -d <design> -m <f1> <f2> <f3>` creates the whole
  directory in one call, or writes nothing at all.
- `lore doctrine edit <name> -m <one-file>` replaces one mission and leaves every
  other file in the directory byte-identical.
- `lore doctrine edit <name> --remove-mission a b` leaves `missions/a.md.deleted`
  and `missions/b.md.deleted` on disk and no live `a.md` or `b.md`.
- `lore new mission ... -D <doctrine>/<mission>` stores the reference verbatim;
  `lore show <mission-id>` prints its body under `--- Mission Instructions ---`.
- `lore show <mission-id>` on an unresolvable reference exits 0, prints no
  warning, and `--json` carries `"doctrine_mission_contents": null`.
- `lore knight` is not a command: `lore knight list` exits 2 with Click's
  `No such command 'knight'.`
- `lore health --scope knights` exits 2 with Click's
  `Error: Invalid value for '--scope': 'knights' is not one of ...`.
- `lore health --scope doctrines` reports an unresolvable `doctrine_mission` on
  an active mission as an ERROR and exits 1; it reports an old-shape doctrine as
  a WARNING and exits 0 on that alone.
- `grep -rn "knight" src/lore/ --include='*.py'` returns nothing.
- A v6 database opened by this release reports `schema_version` 7, has no
  `missions.knight` column, has `missions.doctrine_mission`, and holds no row
  whose `mission_type` is `knight`.

### In Scope / Out of Scope

**In scope:** everything the PRD's MVP section lists, plus three surfaces the
PRD does not name and this spec adds because the change breaks them otherwise:

- `src/lore/oracle.py` — it reads `mission["knight"]` at three sites and renders
  a `Knight` column and a `**Knight:**` line. The column rename makes those a
  lookup error, so oracle is in the core lane.
- `src/lore/prompts.py` — `ACCESS_SCOPE_NOTE` names knights in a user-visible
  `lore init` prompt and `DEFAULT_SKILL_FAMILIES`' docstring says "the five
  skills that do", which becomes four. prompts.py is in the surface lane.
- An explicit retired-seed-tree removal in `src/lore/init.py`. Deleting the
  `knights` row from `SEEDED_TREES` does **not** remove `.lore/knights/default/**`
  on upgrade — `_prune_seeded_tree` only walks trees still listed — so FR-24's
  first half needs a mechanism that does not exist today. See Draft ADR 4.

**Out of scope.** The PRD's Out of Scope list is a floor. A dev knight builds
none of the following, however natural it looks while the file is open:

- **Lore does not execute a doctrine.** No dispatcher, no step runner, no
  "next mission" query over a doctrine. Lore stores prose and surfaces it.
- **No machine-readable step graph, in any file, under any key.** No step ids,
  no `type`, no `phase`, no `needs`, no ordering field. `_validate_steps`,
  `_check_cycles`, `_normalize`, `load_doctrine`, `validate_doctrine_content`
  and `scaffold_doctrine` are **deleted**, not relocated and not reimplemented
  under another name. If a dev knight finds itself writing a topological sort in
  `doctrine.py`, it has left the spec.
- **No `lore doctrine mission` subcommand group.** Per-mission create, replace
  and remove are flags on `lore doctrine edit` and nothing else.
- **No `doctrine-mission` kind in `frontmatter_edit`.** A mission file's
  frontmatter is edited by supplying a replacement file. This is distinct from
  the pre-existing `doctrine` kind, which is repointed onto the design document
  and kept (R19a).
- **No migration of authored knights into doctrine missions.** Lore names the
  orphaned files in the upgrade report and leaves them on disk. Converting them
  is the project's editorial work, not code.
- **No compatibility shim.** No `lore knight` alias, no deprecated `knight=`
  keyword, no `.yaml` reader kept "just in case". Removal is outright.
- **No changes to quest and mission management** beyond the two mission flags
  named in R13. `lore new`, `claim`, `done`, `block`, `needs`, `ready`, `board`
  and the priority model keep their current behaviour.
- **No new doctrine authoring beyond the four seeded ones.** The scribe converts
  the four that ship; it does not invent a fifth.
- **No repair of the codex staleness recon found** (`tech-cli-entity-crud-matrix`
  on codex CRUD, `tech-arch-source-layout`'s three missing modules,
  `preexisting-bugs-handoff` bug #1, the decision-006 numbering collision) beyond
  the rows this feature already has to touch. Those are named in Part 3 for the
  scribe, not for a dev knight. This exclusion covers staleness that exists
  **today**. It does not cover a document this feature's own code change
  falsifies — every one of those is scribe work and is listed in Part 3's
  Expected Codex Updates, the block the ADR & Standards Audit added included.

### Workflows

Every command below is the exact invocation, and every block is the exact
output, character for character. `⏎` is not printed; it marks where a line ends
with no trailing content.

---

**W1 — Read a doctrine to plan a quest** (FR-5, FR-7, FR-8)

```
$ lore doctrine show tdd-feature-lite
---
id: tdd-feature-lite
title: TDD Feature Lite
summary: A three-mission TDD cycle.
---

# TDD Feature Lite

## Missions

- **recon** — map the codex and the code surface
- **feature-spec** — write the one planning document
- **scribe** — reconcile the codex

--- Missions ---
feature-spec  Write the feature spec
recon         Map the codex and the binding decisions
scribe        Reconcile the codex against what shipped
```

Exit 0. The design document is printed **verbatim, whole file, frontmatter
included** — unchanged from today's behaviour for the `design` key. Then one
blank line, then `--- Missions ---`, then one row per mission sorted by id, with
the id left-padded to the widest id and two spaces before the title.

A doctrine with no `missions/` directory still resolves:

```
$ lore doctrine show update-changelog
---
id: update-changelog
...design body...

--- Missions ---
(none)
```

Exit 0.

```
$ lore --json doctrine show tdd-feature-lite
{"doctrine": {"id": "tdd-feature-lite", "title": "TDD Feature Lite", "summary": "A three-mission TDD cycle.", "design": "---\nid: tdd-feature-lite\n...", "missions": [{"id": "feature-spec", "title": "Write the feature spec", "summary": "..."}, {"id": "recon", "title": "Map the codex and the binding decisions", "summary": "..."}, {"id": "scribe", "title": "Reconcile the codex against what shipped", "summary": "..."}], "origin": "self"}}
```

`raw_yaml` and `steps` are gone. The envelope is wrapped under a `doctrine` key.

---

**W2 — Read one mission body** (FR-6, FR-7)

```
$ lore doctrine show tdd-feature-lite --mission recon
# Recon

You are Recon. You map the ground before anyone builds on it.

...body verbatim, frontmatter stripped...
```

Exit 0.

```
$ lore --json doctrine show tdd-feature-lite --mission recon
{"mission": {"id": "recon", "title": "Map the codex and the binding decisions", "summary": "...", "body": "# Recon\n\nYou are Recon...\n"}}
```

Doctrine resolves, mission does not:

```
$ lore doctrine show tdd-feature-lite --mission nope
Mission "nope" not found in doctrine "tdd-feature-lite"
```

stderr, exit 1. With `--json`, stderr carries
`{"error": "Mission \"nope\" not found in doctrine \"tdd-feature-lite\""}`.

Doctrine does not resolve:

```
$ lore doctrine show nope --mission recon
Doctrine 'nope' not found
```

stderr, exit 1 — today's message and code, unchanged. See **Conflict C-1**: the
PRD renders this message with double quotes while the live code uses single
quotes and the PRD's own instruction is "unchanged". This spec ships the live
text pending the gate's ruling.

