**W8 — Upgrade a project** (FR-23, FR-24)

```
$ lore init --yes
...
  Removed knights/default/changelog-scribe.md — no longer shipped
  Removed knights/default/feature-implementation/architect.md — no longer shipped
  ...
  Knights outside default/ are no longer read by Lore: .lore/knights/my-reviewer.md
  Removed doctrines/default/update-changelog.yaml — no longer shipped
  Created doctrines/default/update-changelog/update-changelog.design.md
  Created doctrines/default/update-changelog/missions/update-changelog-entry.md
  ...
```

Exit 0. The database migrates v6→v7 on the first connection. Authored knights
outside `default/` are left on disk and named once, on one line listing every
such path.

### Requirements

| # | Requirement | Type |
|---|-------------|------|
| R1 | A doctrine is a directory named for its stem, holding `<stem>.design.md` and an optional `missions/` directory of `.md` files. | functional (FR-1) |
| R2 | A doctrine's group is derived from the parent of the doctrine **directory**, so `lore doctrine list --filter` behaves exactly as today. | functional (FR-2) |
| R3 | A doctrine mission file carries `id`, `title`, `summary` frontmatter validated by a packaged `doctrine-mission-frontmatter` schema, and its `id` equals its filename stem. | functional (FR-3) |
| R4 | No file in a doctrine carries step order, step type, phase or dependencies, and Lore parses no workflow structure from a doctrine. | functional (FR-4) |
| R5 | An agent reads a doctrine's design and mission index in one command. | functional (FR-5) |
| R6 | An agent reads one mission body by doctrine id and mission id. | functional (FR-6) |
| R7 | Both retrievals are available on the CLI and through `lore.api` with identical return shapes. | functional (FR-7, ADR-011) |
| R8 | A doctrine is addressed by id, never by file path. | functional (FR-8, ADR-006) |
| R9 | An agent creates a complete doctrine — design plus one or more missions — in a single atomic call. | functional (FR-9) |
| R10 | An agent replaces the design, replaces or adds individual missions, and removes missions in a single call, without re-supplying untouched files. | functional (FR-10) |
| R11 | A removed mission is soft-deleted to `<id>.md.deleted`. | functional (FR-11, ADR-003) |
| R12 | A doctrine always has at least one mission; create and edit both enforce it. | functional (FR-12) |
| R13 | A Lore mission records `<doctrine-id>/<mission-id>`, stored verbatim and never interpreted at write time. | functional (FR-13, ADR-004) |
| R14 | `lore show <mission-id>` resolves that reference at read time and splices the body into its output. | functional (FR-14) |
| R15 | An unresolvable reference yields null contents, exit 0 and no output; `lore health` reports it. | functional (FR-15) |
| R16 | No `lore knight` command exists. | functional (FR-16) |
| R17 | No knight name appears in `lore.api.__all__`. | functional (FR-17) |
| R18 | No knight schema ships and no knight files are seeded. | functional (FR-18) |
| R19 | `lore health` offers no `knights` scope. | functional (FR-19) |
| R19a | `lore doctrine edit --set/--unset/--add/--remove` keeps working, now against the design document, with schema `doctrine-design-frontmatter`. | functional (FR-19a) |
| R19b | `decisions-006-id-references` is edited in place with a dated status-history line; its decision is unchanged and it is not marked superseded. | functional (FR-19b) |
| R20 | `mission_type` uses `agent`, `constable` and `human`; `knight` is not a token any seeded doctrine, skill or document uses. | functional (FR-20) |
| R21 | `lore health --scope doctrines` reports old-shape doctrines and stray doctrine YAML as warnings. | functional (FR-21) |
| R22 | `lore health --scope doctrines` reports an unresolvable `doctrine_mission` on an active mission as an error. | functional (FR-22) |
| R23 | The v6→v7 migration drops `missions.knight`, adds `missions.doctrine_mission TEXT`, and rewrites `mission_type` `knight` to `agent`. | functional (FR-23) |
| R24 | `reconcile` removes seeded knights, leaves authored knights on disk, and names both outcomes in its report. | functional (FR-24) |
| R25 | A `mission-design.md` artifact ships under `src/lore/defaults/artifacts/lore-design-documents/`. | functional (FR-25) |
| R26 | `doctrine-design.md` is rewritten — no Knight column, no New Knights section, Type tokens `agent \| constable \| human`, and a Missions section. | functional (FR-26) |
| R27 | The four seeded doctrines ship in the new shape, each former knight persona merged into the mission file that used it. | functional (FR-27) |
| R28 | `skills-catalogue.yaml` retires `update-knight` into `update-doctrine`; the machinery family carries four skills. | functional (FR-28) |
| R29 | `start-quest`, `update-doctrine`, `inquest` and `sync-codex-guide` are updated for the new shape; `update-knight` is removed. | functional (FR-29) |
| R30 | `LORE-AGENT.md`, `GETTING-STARTED.md` and the repository's own agent instruction file describe six entity types and the `agent` mission type. | functional (FR-30) |
| R31 | `src/lore/oracle.py` renders the doctrine-mission reference where it rendered the knight. | functional (spec addition) |
| R32 | `src/lore/prompts.py`'s `ACCESS_SCOPE_NOTE` and `DEFAULT_SKILL_FAMILIES` docstring stop naming knights and say four skills. | functional (spec addition) |
| N1 | An orchestrator reads a doctrine in 1 command; a worker receives description and instructions in 1 command. | non-functional (ADR-001) |
| N2 | Authoring a complete doctrine is 1 command regardless of mission count; replacing one mission is 1 command. | non-functional |
| N3 | `lore doctrine new` and `lore doctrine edit` write nothing when validation fails; `lore doctrine new` additionally leaves no partial directory on disk under any failure, including a crash mid-write. | non-functional |
| N4 | A reference containing `..`, a path separator where none is allowed, or an absolute path resolves to nothing rather than escaping `.lore/`. | non-functional |
| N5 | The v6→v7 migration runs inside the existing chain and is idempotent under the version gate. | non-functional |
| N6 | Cross-project reads stay read-only: every doctrine write function calls `projects.reject_foreign` first. | non-functional (ADR-025) |

---

## Part 2 — Architecture

### Core Decisions

| # | Decision | Choice | Rationale | Requirement |
|---|----------|--------|-----------|-------------|
| D1 | How a doctrine is found on disk | A directory `D` under `.lore/doctrines/` is a doctrine iff `D/<D.name>.design.md` exists. Any path segment starting with `.` or ending `.deleted` is skipped at every level. | Directory identity replaces the `.design.md`+`.yaml` pairing. The skip rule is what makes the staged-write temp directory (D8) and soft-deleted doctrines invisible to discovery in the same one line. | R1, R11 |
| D2 | How the group is derived | `derive_group(doctrine_dir, doctrines_dir)` — the **directory**, not the design file. | `derive_group` takes `relative.parts[:-1]`; passing the design file would fold the doctrine id into the group and break every `--filter` expectation. Passing the directory yields exactly today's answer. No change to `paths.derive_group` itself. | R2 |
| D3 | What `filename` means in a list record | Still `"<stem>.design.md"`. | `scoped.select(..., alias=_doctrine_stem)` and `_doctrine_stem` both key off that string. Keeping it means the nested-projects resolution path needs no change at all. | R7 |
| D4 | What `read_doctrine` returns on a mission miss | A bare `None` means **the doctrine** missed. When the doctrine resolves and the named mission does not, the returned dict carries `"mission": None`. | Settled by the PRD. It is what lets `cli.doctrine_show` emit two different messages from the return value alone, which ADR-011 requires and which forbids the CLI doing its own existence check. | R7 |
| D5 | Where multi-mission validation lives | Entirely in `doctrine.py`. `cli.py` reads files and formats; it validates nothing the module does not. | ADR-011 and `standards-separation-of-concerns`. | R7, R9 |
| D6 | Duplicate `-m` stems | Detected by `doctrine.load_mission_sources(paths)`, a module-level helper that turns a list of paths into the `{id: content}` mapping and raises on a duplicate stem or a missing file. | A `dict` cannot represent a duplicate key, so a Python caller passing `missions={...}` physically cannot express the mistake — the same reasoning `cli.py:299-304` already records for `--project`. Putting the path→content step in `doctrine.py` gives the rule a module home anyway, so nothing lives only in `cli.py`. | R9, ADR-011 |
| D7 | Frontmatter shape rules | JSON Schema only: `doctrine-design-frontmatter` for the design, the new `doctrine-mission-frontmatter` for each mission file. Cross-field rules (`id` == command argument, `id` == filename stem) stay inline in `doctrine.py`. | `standards-dry` — shape validation lives in JSON Schema, never inline; the split mirrors today's `_validate_yaml_schema` / id-match split exactly. | R3 |
| D8 | Atomicity of `lore doctrine new` | Validate everything, build the complete tree in `<doctrines_dir>/.<name>.lore-tmp/`, write each file through `safewrite.atomic_write_text`, then `os.replace(staging, target)`. | The PRD promises "no partial doctrine is ever left on disk". A single directory rename delivers that literally, including under a crash mid-write, and it is six lines. `doctrine.py` becomes the first entity module to use `lore.safewrite`. See Draft ADR 3. | N3, R9 |
| D9 | Atomicity of `lore doctrine edit` | Validate everything first, then per-file `safewrite.atomic_write_text` and per-file `rename` for soft-deletes. No directory swap. | The PRD's own reliability wording scopes the guarantee to validation failure: "a validation failure leaves the tree exactly as it was". A directory swap on edit would have to copy the whole tree including `.deleted` tombstones to preserve them, for a crash window nobody has reported. Stated as the limit of the guarantee rather than left implied. | N3, R10 |
| D10 | Soft-deleting a whole doctrine | `lore doctrine delete <name>` renames the **directory** `<name>/` → `<name>.deleted/`. Envelope `{"id", "deleted": true, "deleted_at": null}` unchanged. | ADR-003 requires a `.deleted` rename for a file entity; the directory is now the entity. D1's skip rule makes the renamed directory invisible to discovery and to health without a second rule. | R11, ADR-003 |
| D11 | The stored reference resolver | Two resolvers, the dual pattern `knight.py` already proved: `_find_doctrine_dir(root, stem)` is strict (rejects `/` and `\` — user input), `_resolve_doctrine_mission(root, ref)` is permissive (accepts exactly one `/`, refuses absolute paths and any `..` segment — stored input). | Directly inherited from `_find_knight` / `_resolve_knight_ref`, which is the fix `preexisting-bugs-handoff` bug #1 led to. Re-deriving it would reintroduce that bug in a new place. | R13, N4 |
| D12 | When a reference resolves | Read time, never write time. `create_mission`/`update_mission` store the string and do not touch the filesystem. | ADR-004's storage posture, and what makes a doctrine edited after a mission was created change what that mission's worker reads. | R13 |
| D13 | `lore show` on an unresolvable reference | Silent. No warning line, exit 0, `doctrine_mission_contents: null`. | Settled by the PRD and the mission brief. It deletes the `Warning: knight file ... not found` line that exists today, so it is a deliberate removal, not an omission. `lore health --scope doctrines` is the single reporter. See Draft ADR 5. | R15 |
| D14 | Where the design body is cut for display | Nowhere. `read_doctrine`'s `design` key is the whole file text, frontmatter included, exactly as today. Only the `--mission` body is frontmatter-stripped, which the PRD states explicitly. | The PRD says "frontmatter stripped" for the mission and does not say it for the design. Changing the design's rendering would break `test_doctrine_show.py` for no stated requirement. | R5 |
| D15 | The `doctrine show --json` envelope | Wrapped: `{"doctrine": {...}}` and `{"mission": {...}}`. Today's is flat. | The PRD specifies the exact envelope and the mission brief makes it authoritative on exactly that. `lore rite show` already wraps (`{"rites": [...]}`), so the shape is not novel. Recorded here because it is a breaking change to an envelope nothing but prior code pinned. | R5, R6 |
| D16 | JSON errors go to stderr on both `--json` paths | `doctrine show`'s local-`--json` branch currently writes its error envelope to **stdout** (`cli.py:2057`); it moves to stderr. **`doctrine_edit` is worse and is also corrected here:** its whole-file branch writes *every* error to stdout in both modes — `File not found: <path>` (`cli.py:2191-2195`), `No content provided on stdin.` (`cli.py:2202-2206`) and the `update_doctrine` `ValueError` (`cli.py:2213-2217`) all call `click.echo(msg)` with no `err=True`. The rewritten `doctrine edit` handler routes every error to stderr, text and JSON alike, matching its `doctrine new` and `doctrine delete` siblings. | `conceptual-workflows-error-handling`: "Error messages go to stderr (`click.echo(..., err=True)`)" and "In JSON mode, error envelopes are also written to stderr." Both of today's deviations are pre-existing; the PRD's "stderr carries `{"error": …}`" is the correction, and this feature rewrites both handlers, so carrying the deviation forward would be a new choice rather than an inherited one. | R6, R10 |
| D17 | Health scope split | `--scope schemas` validates both doctrine frontmatter schemas through the `_SCHEMA_KINDS` table. `--scope doctrines` audits only what JSON Schema cannot express: id-vs-stem, duplicate ids, old-shape leftovers, dangling references, broken artifact refs. | `standards-dry` gives shape validation one home, and `health_check` dedups only `dangling_codex_rite` — running the same validation under two scopes would print every schema error twice on a full `lore health`. See **Conflict C-5**: the mission brief's wording can be read as putting schema validation under `doctrines`. | R21, R22 |
| D18 | `broken_artifact_ref` survives | The check moves from YAML step `notes:` to the design body and every `missions/*.md` body, same regex `\bfi-[a-z0-9-]+\b`, same ERROR severity. Detail becomes `'<id>' not found (mission <mission-id>)`, or `(design)` for a hit in the design body. | The PRD is silent, and silence is not a removal instruction. This is a live capability; the same reasoning the mission brief applies to `frontmatter_edit`'s `doctrine` kind applies here. | R21 |
| D19 | `frontmatter_edit`'s `doctrine` kind | Repointed onto `<doctrine>/<stem>.design.md`, `schema_kind="doctrine-design-frontmatter"`, `shape="md_fm"`, `extension=".md"`, new locator `_doctrine_design_locator`. The `knight` kind and its import are deleted. | R19a, settled by the mission brief. | R19a |
| D20 | Path helpers | `paths.py` gains `DESIGN_SUFFIX`, `MISSIONS_DIRNAME`, `doctrine_design_path(doctrine_dir)`, `doctrine_missions_dir(doctrine_dir)` and `doctrine_mission_path(doctrine_dir, mission_id)`. `knights_dir` and the `"knight"` row in `_ENTITY_LOCATION_BASES` are deleted. | Recon marked the helpers "not strictly required". Verified: `doctrine.py` builds paths inline today, but `health.py` now needs the same three calculations, and `standards-dry` gives that one home. These take a doctrine directory rather than a project root; `derive_group` and `group_matches_filter` already set that precedent in this module. | R1, R3 |
| D21 | Migration mechanism | `v6_to_v7.py` uses the rename-create-copy-drop pattern, recreating `missions` with `doctrine_mission TEXT` where `knight` was, copying rows with `knight` mapped to `doctrine_mission`, then `UPDATE missions SET mission_type = 'agent' WHERE mission_type = 'knight'`, then recreating both indexes. | `v4_to_v5.py` is the direct precedent and does exactly this; `ALTER TABLE … DROP COLUMN` needs SQLite ≥ 3.35 and would leave column order disagreeing with `schema.sql`. Carrying `knight` values across into `doctrine_mission` preserves the operator's data rather than discarding it — an old bare knight name becomes an unresolvable reference that `lore health` reports, which is the designed outcome for a reference that does not resolve. | R23 |
| D22 | Multi-value flag mechanism | `-m` on both `doctrine new` and `doctrine edit`, and `--remove-mission` on `doctrine edit`, all use `cls=SpaceSeparatedChoice, multiple=True` with the default string type. | ADR-012's **decision** is space-separated, never repeatable; both mechanisms deliver it. `doctrine edit` carries two multi-value flags and therefore requires `SpaceSeparatedChoice`; using it on `doctrine new` too keeps one flag spelled one way across a command group, and avoids `lore doctrine new -m a.md b.md tdd` binding `a.md` as `NAME` under the trailing-variadic shape. `SpaceSeparatedChoice` changes the parser, not the validator, so it works with an unconstrained type. | R9, R10, ADR-012 |
| D23 | `--mission` is single-value | Plain `@click.option("--mission", default=None)`. | The PRD states it explicitly; ADR-012 governs multi-value flags and does not apply. | R6 |
| D24 | Removing a retired seed tree | A new `RETIRED_SEED_TREES` tuple in `init.py` naming `.lore/knights/default`, walked on every run, every file unlinked and reported `Removed knights/default/<path> — no longer shipped`, then empty directories pruned. Authored knights outside `default/` are collected and named on one line. | **Verified against source and recon was wrong by omission here.** `_prune_seeded_tree` only walks trees still in `SEEDED_TREES`; deleting the `knights` row means nothing ever looks at `.lore/knights/default/` again and the seeded knights stay on disk forever, contradicting FR-24. See Draft ADR 4. | R24 |
| D25 | `mission_type` token swap | Seeded content and documentation only. No enum, no `CHECK`, no `click.Choice`, no validator. | ADR-004: `mission_type` is stored and exposed, never interpreted. `v4_to_v5.py` already stripped the enum and the CHECK constraint. The only code that touches the token is the migration's one-row-class `UPDATE`. | R20 |
| D26 | Where `api.py`'s knight lines are deleted | In the core lane's excision unit, together with `knight.py` itself. **`Doctrine` and `DoctrineStep` leave `api.py` in the same unit**, for the same reason: C2 deletes them from `models.py`, and `api.py:15` imports them from `models.py`. | `api.py` is a pure re-export facade; a re-export of a deleted module — or of a deleted dataclass — is an `ImportError` at import time, not a lint warning. Every other `api.py` change is the surface lane's. | R17 |
| D27 | Who resolves the reference for `lore show` | `db.get_mission_detail` alone. `cli._show_mission` renders `mission["doctrine_mission_contents"]` and performs no lookup of its own; `cli.py` imports nothing from `lore.doctrine` for this. | **Pre-existing breach being closed, not a new rule.** Today `_show_mission` (`cli.py:2675-2693`) calls `knight_module.read_knight(...)` itself — a second resolution of the same reference the envelope at `cli.py:2603` already carries — and the `Warning: knight file "…" not found` branch exists **only** in `cli.py`, invisible to every `lore.api` caller. `decisions-011` ("no behaviour may live only in `cli.py`"), `standards-separation-of-concerns` ("`cli.py` formats and exits") and `standards-dry` (one resolution home) all forbid it. The fix becomes available for free here because D13 deletes the warning branch and W6 drops the re-rendered frontmatter block, so the body string is the whole of what the CLI needs. | R14, R15 |
| D28 | `-f/--from` and the stdin path leave `lore doctrine edit` | Both are removed. Whole-file mode is `-d`, `-m` and `--remove-mission` only. `lore doctrine edit <name> < file.yaml` stops working; there is no replacement stdin path. | `-f` named the deleted `<name>.yaml`, and a doctrine is now 1 + N files, which one unnamed stdin stream cannot address. Stated explicitly because the CLI Surface tables show the new flags but never say the old ones are gone, and a dev knight reading only those tables would keep `-f` alive beside `-d`. This is a user-visible CLI removal and belongs in the C-4 changelog notice beside the API removals. `-f` on `doctrine new` goes the same way (S4 already says so). | R10 |
| D29 | How `update_doctrine` and `delete_doctrine` find the doctrine | Both go through `_find_doctrine_dir`, which searches the subtree and prefers the shallower match. | **Pre-existing breach being closed.** Today `update_doctrine` looks only at `doctrines_dir / f"{name}.yaml"` (`doctrine.py:358-361`) — flat, no `rglob` — while `delete_doctrine` and `read_doctrine` both resolve recursively. So `lore doctrine edit` on any doctrine under `default/` already fails with `Doctrine "x" not found.` today. After FR-27 every seeded doctrine lives under `default/`, so E17–E25 and E26 are unreachable unless the lookup is recursive. `standards-dry` — one resolver, used by every write function in the module. | R10, R2 |
| D30 | `safewrite` calls pass `project_root` | Every `safewrite.atomic_write_text(...)` call in `doctrine.py` passes `project_root=project_root`. | `safewrite`'s containment property ("Never outside the project root") is only enforced when the keyword is supplied; with it omitted only the symlink refusal runs. `doctrine.py` writes paths built from a user-supplied name and group, which is the case the property exists for. | N3, N4 |

### Data and Storage

**On disk.** A doctrine is:

```
.lore/doctrines/<group…>/<stem>/
├── <stem>.design.md          # required — frontmatter id/title/summary + prose
└── missions/                 # optional
    ├── <mission-id>.md       # frontmatter id/title/summary + prose
    └── <mission-id>.md.deleted   # soft-deleted, invisible to every read
```

`<group…>` is zero or more directory levels; the group string is
`derive_group(<stem>-dir, .lore/doctrines)`. Seeded doctrines live under
`default/`, which `projects.is_seeded_default` already recognises from the
derived group string alone — the extra nesting level changes nothing there.

`.lore/knights/` is no longer written, read, seeded or gitignored. It is not
deleted when a project authored files there.

**Schema (SQLite).** Migration **v6 → v7**, `SCHEMA_VERSION` 6 → 7.

| Change | Statement |
|--------|-----------|
| Drop `missions.knight` | rename-create-copy-drop (D21) |
| Add `missions.doctrine_mission TEXT` | in the position `knight` held |
| Carry data | `INSERT INTO missions SELECT id, quest_id, title, description, status, priority, knight, block_reason, created_at, updated_at, closed_at, deleted_at, mission_type FROM missions_old` |
| Rewrite the token | `UPDATE missions SET mission_type = 'agent' WHERE mission_type = 'knight'` |
| Recreate indexes | `idx_missions_quest_id`, `idx_missions_status_priority` |

`src/lore/defaults/schema.sql` changes `knight TEXT` → `doctrine_mission TEXT`
and `INSERT INTO lore_meta … '6'` → `'7'`, so a fresh database and a migrated
one are identical.

**Packaged schemas.** 12 → 11.

| File | Action |
|------|--------|
| `src/lore/schemas/doctrine-mission-frontmatter.yaml` | **new** — `$id: lore://schemas/doctrine-mission-frontmatter`, `type: object`, `additionalProperties: false`, `required: [id, title, summary]`, each a `string` with `minLength: 1`; `id`'s description reads "Doctrine mission id. Must match the filename stem." |
| `src/lore/schemas/knight-frontmatter.yaml` | **deleted** |
| `src/lore/schemas/doctrine-yaml.yaml` | **deleted** |
| `src/lore/schemas/doctrine-design-frontmatter.yaml` | edited — `id`'s description stops saying "Must match the paired YAML file's id" and reads "Doctrine id. Must match the doctrine directory name." The required set stays `[id, title, summary]`. **Recon recorded this file as requiring only `id`; verified against source, it requires all three.** |

### Python API Surface

```python
# lore/doctrine.py

def list_doctrines(
    project_root: Path,
    filter_groups: list[str] | None = None,
    *,
    scope: str | None = None,
) -> list[dict]: ...
# [{"id", "group", "title", "summary", "valid", "filename", "origin"}] — keys unchanged.
# "filename" is "<stem>.design.md". Order is path order per project, concatenated
# across projects — unchanged (D-16).

def read_doctrine(
    project_root: Path,
    doctrine_id: str,
    *,
    scope: str | None = None,
    mission: str | None = None,
) -> dict | None: ...
# {"id", "title", "summary", "design", "missions": [{"id","title","summary"}], "origin"}
# plus "mission": {"id","title","summary","body"} | None when mission is not None.
# None means the DOCTRINE missed, and only that.

def create_doctrine(
    project_root: Path,
    name: str,
    design_content: str,
    missions: dict[str, str],
    *,
    group: str | None = None,
) -> dict: ...
# {"created": name, "group": group, "missions": sorted(missions), "path": "<repo-relative>/"}

def update_doctrine(
    project_root: Path,
    name: str,
    design_content: str | None = None,
    missions: dict[str, str] | None = None,
    remove_missions: list[str] | None = None,
) -> dict: ...
# {"updated": name, "design_replaced": bool,
#  "missions_replaced": sorted[...], "missions_removed": sorted[...]}
# Resolves the doctrine directory through _find_doctrine_dir — subtree-wide,
# shallower match wins (D29). Today's flat lookup is a bug; a doctrine under
# default/ cannot be edited at all.

def delete_doctrine(project_root: Path, name: str) -> dict: ...
# {"id": name, "deleted": True, "deleted_at": None} — unchanged

def load_mission_sources(paths: Sequence[Path]) -> dict[str, str]: ...
# Internal. Path list -> {stem: content}. Raises ValueError on a missing file or
# a duplicate stem.

def _find_doctrine_dir(project_root: Path, stem: str) -> Path | None: ...
def _resolve_doctrine_mission(project_root: Path, ref: str) -> Path | None: ...
def _doctrine_mission_stem(ref: str) -> str: ...
# Internal. The strict/permissive resolver pair and the stem extractor (D11).
```

```python
# lore/db.py

def create_mission(
    project_root: Path,
    title: str,
    quest_id: str | None = None,
    description: str = "",
    priority: int = 2,
    doctrine_mission: str | None = None,
    mission_type: str | None = None,
) -> dict: ...

def update_mission(
    project_root: Path,
    mission_id: str,
    title: str | None = None,
    description: str | None = None,
    priority: int | None = None,
    doctrine_mission: str | None = None,
    remove_doctrine_mission: bool = False,
    mission_type: str | None = None,
) -> dict: ...

def update_mission_full(
    project_root: Path,
    mission_id: str,
    *,
    title: str | None = None,
    description: str | None = None,
    priority: int | None = None,
    doctrine_mission: str | None = None,
    remove_doctrine_mission: bool = False,
    mission_type: str | None = None,
) -> dict: ...

def get_mission_detail(
    project_root: Path,
    mission_id: str,
    *,
    include_doctrine_mission: bool = True,
) -> dict | None: ...
```

`get_mission_detail` hydrates through a lazy
`from lore.doctrine import _resolve_doctrine_mission` — the same documented
dependency-inversion exception the lazy `from lore.knight import read_knight`
occupies today, moved one module across. It reads the resolved file and strips
frontmatter; it does not call `read_doctrine`, because it needs one body and not
a whole doctrine record.

```python
# lore/health.py
def _check_doctrines(
    doctrines_dir: Path, artifacts_dir: Path, project_root: Path
) -> list[HealthIssue]: ...
# _check_knights is DELETED. project_root is now a parameter rather than
# derived from knights_dir.parent.parent.
```

```python
# lore/paths.py
DESIGN_SUFFIX = ".design.md"
MISSIONS_DIRNAME = "missions"

def doctrine_design_path(doctrine_dir: Path) -> Path: ...
def doctrine_missions_dir(doctrine_dir: Path) -> Path: ...
def doctrine_mission_path(doctrine_dir: Path, mission_id: str) -> Path: ...
# knights_dir() is DELETED. _ENTITY_LOCATION_BASES loses its "knight" row.
```

| Name | Module | In `lore.api.__all__`? | Raises |
|------|--------|------------------------|--------|
| `list_doctrines` | `lore.doctrine` | yes (kept) | `UnknownProjectError` on an unknown `scope` |
| `read_doctrine` | `lore.doctrine` | yes (kept, new `mission=` keyword) | `ValueError` when `mission` contains a path separator |
| `create_doctrine` | `lore.doctrine` | yes (signature changed) | `ForeignEntityError`; `ValueError` on every condition in the CLI failure table |
| `update_doctrine` | `lore.doctrine` | yes (signature changed) | `ForeignEntityError`; `ValueError` on every condition in the CLI failure table |
| `delete_doctrine` | `lore.doctrine` | yes (unchanged) | `ForeignEntityError`; `ValueError` on a miss |
| `load_mission_sources` | `lore.doctrine` | no (internal) | `ValueError` on a missing file or duplicate stem |
| `_find_doctrine_dir`, `_resolve_doctrine_mission`, `_doctrine_mission_stem` | `lore.doctrine` | no (internal) | `ValueError` on a path separator (strict resolver only) |
| `create_mission`, `update_mission`, `update_mission_full`, `get_mission_detail` | `lore.db` | yes (keywords renamed) | `ValueError` as today |
| `Knight` | `lore.models` | **removed from both `lore.api.__all__` and `lore.models.__all__`** — verified at `api.py:211` and `models.py:474` | — |
| `list_knights`, `read_knight`, `create_knight`, `update_knight`, `delete_knight` | — | **removed** | — |
| `_knight`, `_validate_frontmatter` (private aliases) | — | **removed**; `api._validate_frontmatter` has no caller in `src/` or `tests/` | — |
| `Doctrine`, `DoctrineStep` | `lore.models` | **removed from `lore.api.__all__` AND `lore.models.__all__`** — both are public names today, verified at `api.py:15` (the `from lore.models import …` line), `api.py:208-210` and `models.py:473-474`. Both describe a step graph that no longer exists; `DoctrineListEntry` stays. Deleting them from `models.py` while `api.py` still re-exports them is an `ImportError` at import time — D26's own reasoning about `knight.py` applies unchanged to a deleted dataclass — so both edits land in the same unit (C4). | — |

**Breaking-change accounting (ADR-010).** **Eight** names leave
`lore.api.__all__` — `list_knights`, `read_knight`, `create_knight`,
`update_knight`, `delete_knight`, `Knight`, `Doctrine`, `DoctrineStep` — and
**three** leave `lore.models.__all__` (`Knight`, `Doctrine`, `DoctrineStep`).
Two exported callables change their positional parameter lists
(`create_doctrine`, `update_doctrine`). Four change keyword names
(`create_mission`, `update_mission`, `update_mission_full`,
`get_mission_detail`). One exported dataclass changes a field name
(`Mission.knight` → `Mission.doctrine_mission`).

**Return shapes change on nine exported callables**, which ADR-010's table
scores identically to a positional-list change: `read_doctrine` (drops
`raw_yaml` and `steps`, gains `missions` and the optional `mission` key),
`create_doctrine` (`{id, filename, group, design_filename}` →
`{created, group, missions, path}`), `update_doctrine` (`{id, filename,
updated_at}` → `{updated, design_replaced, missions_replaced,
missions_removed}`), and the six mission-bearing envelopes whose per-mission
`knight` key becomes `doctrine_mission` — `get_mission_detail` (also
`knight_contents` → `doctrine_mission_contents`), `list_missions`,
`list_missions_grouped`, `read_mission`, `get_quest_detail` and
`get_ready_missions`.

Every one of those rows is "major bump **or** explicit breaking-change notice"
in ADR-010's semver table. Separately and unconditionally, ADR-010's
Consequences and `standards-public-api-stability`'s Rules for Contributors both
state that **every release that changes the public API must include a
`CHANGELOG.md` entry** — that obligation does not depend on which branch of the
semver table is taken. `CHANGELOG.md` exists at the repository root and
`pyproject.toml` reads `version = "0.11.0"`. The PRD names v0.12.0 and scopes
no changelog work, and no requirement, lane or unit in this spec produces
either. See **Conflict C-4**.

### CLI Surface

**`lore doctrine show NAME [--mission ID] [--json]`**

```
$ lore doctrine show tdd-feature-lite
<design file, verbatim, whole>

--- Missions ---
feature-spec  Write the feature spec
recon         Map the codex and the binding decisions
```

Renderer, exactly:

```python
click.echo(d["design"], nl=False)
click.echo("")
click.echo("--- Missions ---")
if not d["missions"]:
    click.echo("(none)")
else:
    width = max(len(m["id"]) for m in d["missions"])
    for m in d["missions"]:
        click.echo(f"{m['id'].ljust(width)}  {m['title']}")
```

```json
{"doctrine": {"id": "…", "title": "…", "summary": "…", "design": "…", "missions": [{"id": "…", "title": "…", "summary": "…"}], "origin": "self"}}
```

```json
{"mission": {"id": "recon", "title": "…", "summary": "…", "body": "…"}}
```

| Failure | Message | Exit |
|---------|---------|------|
| Doctrine does not resolve | `Doctrine 'nope' not found` (stderr; see C-1) | 1 |
| Doctrine resolves, mission does not | `Mission "nope" not found in doctrine "tdd-feature-lite"` (stderr) | 1 |
| `--mission` contains `/` or `\` | `Invalid mission id: path separators not allowed` (stderr) | 1 |

**`lore doctrine new NAME -d DESIGN -m FILE [FILE ...] [--group G] [--json]`**

```
Created doctrine tdd-feature-lite with 3 missions in group default
Created doctrine tdd-feature-lite with 1 mission
Created doctrine tdd-feature-lite with 3 missions
```

```json
{"created": "tdd-feature-lite", "group": "default", "missions": ["feature-spec", "recon", "scribe"], "path": ".lore/doctrines/default/tdd-feature-lite/"}
```

Validation order in `create_doctrine`, and the exact message each step raises:

| # | Check | Message | Exit |
|---|-------|---------|------|
| 0 | `projects.reject_foreign(name)` | `Cannot write "camelot:x": an entity from another project is read-only.` | 1 |
| 1 | `validate_name(name)` | `Invalid name: must start with alphanumeric and contain only letters, digits, hyphens, underscores.` | 1 |
| 2 | `validate_group(group)` | as `validators.validate_group` returns it | 1 |
| 3 | duplicate anywhere in the subtree | `Error: doctrine 'tdd-feature-lite' already exists at <path>` | 1 |
| 4 | design frontmatter present, `id` matches | `Design file id "x" does not match command argument "tdd-feature-lite"` | 1 |
| 5 | design frontmatter schema | schema issue messages joined by `\n` | 1 |
| 6 | at least one mission | `At least one mission file is required (-m)` | 1 |
| 7 | each mission id is a valid name | `Invalid mission id "bad id": must start with alphanumeric and contain only letters, digits, hyphens, underscores.` | 1 |
| 8 | each mission frontmatter `id` == stem | `Mission file id "x" does not match filename stem "recon"` | 1 |
| 9 | each mission frontmatter schema | `Mission "recon": <issue messages joined by \n>` | 1 |
| — | CLI-only: `-d` absent | `Error: -d/--design is required` | 1 |
| — | CLI-only: a source file is missing | `File not found: /tmp/recon.md` | 1 |
| — | CLI-only (`load_mission_sources`): two `-m` files share a stem | `Duplicate mission id "recon": two -m files share a filename stem` | 1 |

**`lore doctrine edit NAME [-d DESIGN] [-m FILE ...] [--remove-mission ID ...]`**
**`lore doctrine edit NAME [--set K=V] [--unset K] [--add K=V] [--remove K=V]`**

`-f/--from` and the read-from-stdin fallback are **removed** (D28). The
subcommand docstring, which today reads "Field-edit mode (mutually exclusive
with -f / --from) targets the `<name>.yaml` file. To edit the `.design.md`
partner, use `-f`.", is rewritten: field-edit mode is mutually exclusive with
`-d`, `-m` and `--remove-mission`, and targets the design document.

```
Updated doctrine tdd-feature-lite (design replaced)
Updated doctrine tdd-feature-lite (missions replaced: recon)
Updated doctrine tdd-feature-lite (missions removed: lint, refactor)
Updated doctrine tdd-feature-lite (design replaced; missions replaced: feature-spec, recon; missions removed: lint)
Updated doctrine tdd-feature-lite
```

The last form is field-edit mode, unchanged.

```json
{"updated": "tdd-feature-lite", "design_replaced": true, "missions_replaced": ["recon"], "missions_removed": []}
```

| Failure | Message | Exit |
|---------|---------|------|
| Foreign name | `Cannot write "camelot:x": an entity from another project is read-only.` | 1 |
| Doctrine miss | `Doctrine "nope" not found.` (trailing period — today's text) | 1 |
| No `-d`, `-m` or `--remove-mission` | `Nothing to update: pass -d, -m, or --remove-mission` | 1 — see C-2 |
| Whole-file flags mixed with field-edit flags | today's `_reject_mutex` message, unchanged | 1 |
| `--remove-mission` names a missing mission | `Mission "lint" not found in doctrine "tdd-feature-lite"` | 1 |
| The removal would empty the doctrine | `Cannot remove every mission: a doctrine keeps at least one mission.` | 1 |
| A `-m` file fails schema or id-vs-stem | as the `doctrine new` table, rows 7–9 | 1 |
| `-d` file fails schema or id match | as the `doctrine new` table, rows 4–5 | 1 |

**`lore doctrine list`** — unchanged in every respect: same columns, same JSON,
same `--filter` behaviour, same ordering. Only discovery underneath changes.

**`lore doctrine delete NAME`** — unchanged message `Deleted doctrine <name>`
and envelope; the directory is renamed rather than two files (D10).

**`lore new mission … -D REF`** and **`lore edit MISSION -D REF | --no-doctrine-mission`**

`-k/--knight` and `--no-knight` are removed. Help text:
`-D, --doctrine-mission TEXT   Doctrine mission reference <doctrine>/<mission>.`
and `--no-doctrine-mission  Remove doctrine mission assignment.`

| Failure | Message | Exit |
|---------|---------|------|
| Both `-D` and `--no-doctrine-mission` | `Error: --doctrine-mission and --no-doctrine-mission are mutually exclusive.` | 2 |
| `lore edit` with no field flags | `Error: At least one of --title, --description, --priority, --doctrine-mission, --no-doctrine-mission, --auto-close, --no-auto-close, or --type is required.` | 2 |

**`lore show MISSION [--no-doctrine-mission] [--json]`**

The `Knight: <x>` line becomes `Doctrine Mission: <x>`. The
`--- Knight Contents ---` section becomes `--- Mission Instructions ---` and
prints the resolved body alone, with `nl=False`. The `--no-knight` flag becomes
`--no-doctrine-mission`, help `Omit doctrine mission instructions.` The JSON
envelope replaces `knight`/`knight_contents` with
`doctrine_mission`/`doctrine_mission_contents`.

The body the section prints is `mission["doctrine_mission_contents"]` — the key
`get_mission_detail` already returns. `_show_mission` performs **no** resolution
of its own and `cli.py` gains no `lore.doctrine` import for it (D27). The
section is emitted when that value is non-null and `--no-doctrine-mission` was
not passed; a null value emits nothing at all, which is D13's silence.

**`lore missions`, `lore ready`, `lore show QUEST`, `lore oracle`** — the
`knight` JSON key becomes `doctrine_mission`; the text-mode bracketed suffix
`  [<ref>]` keeps its exact form; oracle's `| Knight |` column header becomes
`| Doctrine Mission |` and its `**Knight:** x` line becomes
`**Doctrine Mission:** x`.

**`lore health --scope …`** — the `knights` token leaves both
`health._ALL_SCOPES` and `cli._VALID_SCOPES`. New rows under `doctrines`:

| severity | entity_type | id | check | detail |
|----------|-------------|-----|-------|--------|
| warning | doctrines | `<stem>` | `missing_missions_dir` | `no missions/ directory — this doctrine is not readable in the current shape` |
| warning | doctrines | `<stem>` | `stray_yaml` | `<stem>.yaml is no longer read` |
| warning | doctrines | `<dirname>` | `missing_design` | `no <dirname>.design.md — this directory is not a doctrine` |
| error | doctrines | `<stem>` | `id_mismatch` | `design frontmatter id '<id>' does not match directory name` |
| error | doctrines | `<stem>/<mission>` | `mission_id_mismatch` | `frontmatter id '<id>' does not match filename stem '<mission>'` |
| error | doctrines | `<id>` | `duplicate_id` | `declared by <pathA> and <pathB>` |
| error | doctrines | `<doctrine>/<mission>` | `missing_file` | `referenced by q-a1b2/m-c3d4, q-a1b2/m-e5f6 but not found on disk` |
| error | doctrines | `<stem>` | `broken_artifact_ref` | `'fi-x' not found (mission recon)` or `'fi-x' not found (design)` |

`orphaned_file` and `broken_knight_ref` are removed. A directory holding neither
`missions/` nor `<name>.design.md` is a grouping directory and produces nothing.

**`lore --help`** — the entity block drops the `Knight` line, leaving **five**
supporting-entity lines (Doctrine, Codex, Rite, Artifact, Watcher) below the two
core lines (Quest, Mission), for **seven** named entities in the block. Verified
against `cli.py`'s top-level docstring, which lists six supporting entities
today. ADR-008: the group and top-level help teach the entity model, so an
entity that no longer exists cannot stay listed.

> Do not conflate this count with FR-30's "six entity types". That figure is the
> PRD's Technical Success metric (7 → 6) and counts Quest, Mission, Doctrine,
> Rite, Artifact and Watcher — Codex is excluded from it. The help block counts
> Codex. The two numbers describe different sets and are both correct.

**`lore doctrine --help`** — the group paragraph is rewritten: a doctrine is a
directory of prose, not a YAML step graph; `lore doctrine show <id>` returns the
design and the mission index; `--mission <id>` returns one mission body; an
orchestrator decides order and type from the design prose.

**`lore init --help`** — the paragraph at `cli.py:738-747` stops naming knights
in "A skill, knight, doctrine, artifact or watcher Lore shipped…" and in
"Knights and the rest are seeded under a default/ subdirectory".

### Project Structure

Every file this feature creates, modifies or deletes, with the rule that binds
it written out.

**Foundation lane**

| Path | New/Modified | Binding rule, stated |
|------|--------------|---------------------|
| `src/lore/schemas/doctrine-mission-frontmatter.yaml` | **new** | `standards-dry` — a frontmatter *shape* rule lives in JSON Schema and nowhere else; the cross-field `id == stem` rule does not go here because JSON Schema cannot see the filename. |
| `src/lore/schemas/__init__.py` | modified | `_FRONTMATTER_KINDS` gains `doctrine-mission-frontmatter`. In the core lane's excision unit it loses `knight-frontmatter` and `_YAML_KINDS` loses `doctrine-yaml`. `tech-arch-schemas` documents this dispatch table; `standards-dependency-inversion` — this module imports only `lore.paths`. |
| `src/lore/schemas/doctrine-design-frontmatter.yaml` | modified | Description text only. The required set stays `[id, title, summary]` — `doctrine.py` raises the id-presence error before full validation and depends on that ordering. |
| `src/lore/paths.py` | modified | `standards-dependency-inversion` — `paths.py` imports nothing from `lore.*`, so the new helpers take `Path` arguments and never a project object. The magic string `".lore"` is centralised here and must not appear in any other module. |

**Core lane**

| Path | New/Modified | Binding rule, stated |
|------|--------------|---------------------|
| `src/lore/doctrine.py` | **rewritten** | `standards-dependency-inversion` — may import `lore.paths`, `lore.frontmatter`, `lore.validators`, `lore.schemas`, `lore.projects`, `lore.scoped`, `lore.safewrite`; never `lore.cli`, never `lore.db`. `standards-single-responsibility` — it owns the doctrine directory and everything inside it, including mission files; no separate module appears for missions. `decisions-011` — every validation the CLI surfaces is enforced here. `decisions-025` — `projects.reject_foreign(name)` is the first statement of `create_doctrine`, `update_doctrine` and `delete_doctrine`. `decisions-003` — `--remove-mission` renames to `.md.deleted`; it never unlinks. |
| `src/lore/knight.py` | **deleted outright** | `decisions-003` Scope section: this ADR governs entities the `lore` CLI manages, not Lore's own source; deleting a module is a plain hard delete with no soft-delete ceremony. `standards-single-responsibility` — its logic is not folded into `doctrine.py`; the resolver *pattern* is reproduced, the code is not moved. |
| `src/lore/db.py` | modified | `decisions-004` — `mission_type` is stored and exposed, never interpreted: no enum, no CHECK, no validation of the token. `decisions-011` — validation lives here, not in `cli.py`. `standards-dependency-inversion` — the lazy `from lore.doctrine import …` inside `get_mission_detail` is the documented exception and stays a *function-local* import. |
| `src/lore/models.py` | modified | `decisions-010` — a field rename on an exported dataclass and two names leaving `models.__all__` are breaking changes; see Conflict C-4. `Mission.knight` → `doctrine_mission`; `Doctrine` and `DoctrineStep` deleted. |
| `src/lore/migrations/v6_to_v7.py` | **new** | `conceptual-workflows-schema-migrations` — a migration module exposes exactly `migrate(conn)`, is discovered by module name from `db._run_migrations`, and never commits: the runner owns the transaction and the `schema_version` write. |
| `src/lore/defaults/schema.sql` | modified | A fresh database must equal a migrated one; `decisions-006-no-seed-content-tests` does not apply — this is DDL, not seed content. |
| `src/lore/health.py` | modified | `conceptual-workflows-health` — a warning never raises the exit code unless its check is in `_ESCALATED_WARNING_CHECKS`, and nothing here is added to that set. `standards-dry` — schema validation stays in `_check_schemas`/`_SCHEMA_KINDS` (D17). `_check_doctrines` reuses `doctrine._resolve_doctrine_mission` rather than re-deriving reference resolution. |
| `src/lore/frontmatter_edit.py` | modified | `decisions-025` — `_projects.reject_foreign(name)` stays the first statement of `update_frontmatter_fields`. The `knight` kind and `from lore import knight as _knight_mod` are deleted; the `doctrine` kind is repointed (D19) because removing a live capability is not this change's purpose. |
| `src/lore/oracle.py` | modified | `decisions-004` — oracle renders `mission_type` and the reference as opaque strings and interprets neither. **Recon inferred oracle needed no change; verified false** at `oracle.py:161,162,170,182`. |
| `src/lore/init.py` | modified | `decisions-024` — an entity is a seeded default iff its derived group is `default` or starts with `default/`; the install manifest is never consulted for that test. `conceptual-workflows-init-reconcile` — "a path in neither the desired nor the recorded set is never read, never written and never deleted", which is exactly why an authored knight is left alone and why a retired seeded tree needs its own explicit list (D24). |
| `src/lore/defaults/gitignore` | modified | `tech-arch-initialized-project-structure` — the three `knights` lines (`!knights`, `!knights/**`, `knights/default/`) are removed; the `doctrines` triad is unchanged because `doctrines/default/` still matches a directory-of-directories. |
| `src/lore/defaults/knights/**` (12 files) | **deleted** | `decisions-024` — these are seeded defaults; they are Lore's to remove. |
| `tests/conftest.py` | modified | `technical-test-guidelines` §7 — there are exactly three conftest files and shared helpers live in the root one. `insert_mission`'s `knight=` keyword and its `INSERT` column list become `doctrine_mission`. |
| `tests/unit/conftest.py` | modified | Same rule. `bare_lore_dir` drops `knights`; `_ENTITY_DIRS` drops `knights`; `NestedTree.entity` drops the `"knight"` kind; `NestedTree.doctrine` writes the new directory shape. |

**Surface lane**

| Path | New/Modified | Binding rule, stated |
|------|--------------|---------------------|
| `src/lore/api.py` | modified | `standards-facade` — zero `def`, zero `class`; names arrive only as re-exports and new names go in the operational-callables block. `decisions-010` — `__all__` is the contract and its **grouped order** is pinned by `tests/unit/test_api_all_matches_spec.py`, so removing six names means editing that test's literal tuple too. |
| `src/lore/cli.py` | modified | `standards-separation-of-concerns` — `cli.py` formats and exits; no directory-write logic, no duplicate-detection, no schema call. `decisions-008` — the top-level and group `--help` paragraphs teach the entity model and must be updated, while subcommand and flag help stays terse. `decisions-012` — `-m` and `--remove-mission` are space-separated, never repeatable. `decisions-017` — `--scope` keeps `click.Choice`; only the token set shrinks, and the flag's own help string loses its `knights` example. `conceptual-workflows-error-handling` — **errors go to stderr in both modes**, which closes two pre-existing stdout deviations in the handlers this feature rewrites (D16). `decisions-011` + `standards-separation-of-concerns` — `_show_mission` renders the body the envelope already carries and resolves nothing itself (D27). |
| `src/lore/prompts.py` | modified | `conceptual-workflows-init-interactive` — `ACCESS_SCOPE_NOTE` is user-visible prompt text and its two-line wrap is part of the rendering. **Recon marked prompts.py out of scope; verified in scope** at `prompts.py:37` and `prompts.py:41`. |

**Test files deleted outright (8)**

