---

## Part 3 — Decision and Standards Impact

### Binding Table

| Rule id | The rule, in one line | Obligation on the dev knight |
|---------|----------------------|------------------------------|
| `decisions-001-dumb-infrastructure` | Lore stores and answers queries; it never interprets, orchestrates or runs a template engine, and minimising tool calls is a design constraint. | Do not parse, interpret or execute a mission-file body anywhere. `read_doctrine` reads frontmatter for the index and returns the body as an opaque string. Keep `lore doctrine show` a single call that returns design **and** index; do not add a second command to list missions. |
| `decisions-003-soft-delete-semantics` | File entities are soft-deleted by a `.deleted` rename, never unlinked; the ADR's Scope section excludes Lore's own source code. | `--remove-mission` renames `missions/<id>.md` → `missions/<id>.md.deleted`. `lore doctrine delete` renames the directory to `<name>.deleted`. Deleting `src/lore/knight.py`, the 12 seeded knight files and the 8 knight test files is plain `git rm` — no `.deleted` ceremony, no deprecation period. |
| `decisions-004-mission-type-dumb-storage` | `mission_type` is stored and exposed, never interpreted; no enum, no CHECK, no `click.Choice`. | Do not add validation for `agent`. The only code that touches the token is one `UPDATE` inside `v6_to_v7.py`. Do not add a `click.Choice` to `-T/--type`. |
| `decisions-006-id-references` | Agents address entities by ID through the CLI, never by constructed path; the by-ID rule holds in both access modes for artifacts, doctrines and watchers. | Never emit a `.lore/doctrines/...` path in help text, an error message, a seeded skill or a mission-file body as the way to reach a doctrine. A mission-file body that references an artifact names the artifact id and the `lore artifact show <id>` command. The stale enumeration is FR-19b's edit-in-place, the scribe's job. |
| `decisions-006-no-seed-content-tests` | Tests never assert a field *value* or a prose string from `src/lore/defaults/`; existence, parseability and structural-key presence only. | Every test touching the converted doctrines, the new `mission-design.md`, the rewritten `doctrine-design.md`, `skills-catalogue.yaml` or the deleted knights asserts only that a path exists or does not exist, that a file parses, and that required keys are present. No assertion on a doctrine's title, a mission's summary, or the text of a retirement reason. |
| `decisions-008-help-as-teaching-interface` | Top-level and command-group `--help` teach the entity and workflow model; subcommand and flag help stay terse. | Update the top-level entity block (**five** supporting entities after the `Knight` line goes, seven named entities in the block), the `lore doctrine` group paragraph (directory of prose, `--mission`, no step graph) and the `lore init` ownership paragraph. Also fix `--scope`'s own help string on `lore health`, which reads `(space-separated, e.g. --scope codex knights schemas)` — a flag help that demonstrates a token which will exit 2. Keep the new flag help to one line each. Do not put a tutorial on `doctrine new`. |
| `decisions-010-public-api-stability` | `lore.api.__all__` is the sole public contract; removing a name, renaming one, changing a positional list or a return shape is major-bump-or-explicit-breaking-change-notice. | Remove the six knight names *and* update `tests/unit/test_api_all_matches_spec.py`'s literal tuple in the same commit — the test pins membership **and order**. Do not invent a version number; the release decision is Conflict C-4. |
| `decisions-011-api-parity-with-cli` | Every `lore.api` function is self-contained and behaviourally identical to the CLI; no validation or behaviour may live only in `cli.py`. | Multi-mission validation, atomicity, the ≥1-mission rule and the last-mission guard all live in `doctrine.py`. The CLI performs no existence check before calling `read_doctrine`; it distinguishes the two misses from the return value alone (D4). The one CLI-only check, duplicate `-m` stems, is unrepresentable in the Python signature and its helper still lives in `doctrine.py` (D6). |
| `decisions-012-multi-value-cli-param-convention` | Multi-value flags are space-separated, never repeatable. | `lore doctrine new -m a.md b.md c.md`, `lore doctrine edit --remove-mission a b`. Never `-m a.md -m b.md`. `--mission` on `doctrine show` is single-value and outside this rule. |
| `decisions-017-constrained-flags-use-click-choice` | Constrained-value flags use `click.Choice`; the `BadParameter`/stderr/exit-2 behaviour is a pinned contract amendable only in place. | Drop `"knights"` from `_VALID_SCOPES` and `_ALL_SCOPES` and change nothing else about `--scope`. Do not hand-roll a validator, do not reword the message, do not change the exit code. Adding no new token means no ADR amendment is triggered. |
| `decisions-021-health-reports-are-ephemeral-by-default` | `lore health` writes a markdown report only under the `latest` or `all` retention policy. | The new doctrine checks change what the report contains, never whether it is written. Do not touch `_write_report` or `_prune_reports`. |
| `decisions-024-default-group-is-seeded-boundary` | An entity is a seeded default iff its derived group is `default` or starts with `default/`; the install manifest is never consulted for that test. | The extra nesting level (`default/<doctrine>/missions/`) does not change `is_seeded_default`, which reads only the derived group string. Do not add a depth check. The retired-tree removal (D24) is keyed on the path `.lore/knights/default`, which is the same boundary stated as a path. |
| `decisions-025-cross-boundary-reads-are-read-only` | `projects.reject_foreign` is the first statement of every externally callable write on a file-backed entity. | It is the first statement of `create_doctrine`, `update_doctrine` and `delete_doctrine` — before name validation, before any filesystem call, before mission-file validation. |
| `standards-dependency-inversion` | `doctrine.py` may import `paths`, `frontmatter`, `validators` and the other leaf modules — never `cli.py`, never `db.py`. | The rewritten `doctrine.py` gains `lore.safewrite` (a leaf, stdlib-only) and keeps `lore.projects`/`lore.scoped`. It must not gain a `db` import to check which missions are referenced — that direction belongs to `health.py`. `db.get_mission_detail`'s import of `lore.doctrine` stays function-local. |
| `standards-dry` | Doctrine loading and validation have exactly one module home; shape validation lives in JSON Schema, never inline. | One resolver pair in `doctrine.py`, used by `doctrine.py`, `db.py` and `health.py`. One path calculation in `paths.py`, used by `doctrine.py` and `health.py`. No second copy of the mission-file frontmatter rules inside `health.py` (D17). |
| `standards-facade` | `lore.api` is a pure re-export facade — zero `def`, zero `class`. | Do not write a wrapper for the new `mission=` keyword. `read_doctrine` is re-exported as-is. |
| `standards-public-api-stability` | The same semver table as ADR-010, restated as a standards document. | Same obligation; see Conflict C-4. |
| `standards-separation-of-concerns` | `cli.py` formats and exits; validation, atomicity and duplicate detection live in the entity module. | The `doctrine new`/`edit` handlers read files, call one module function, format the message and exit. No `mkdir`, no `write_text`, no `rename`, no `validate_entity` in `cli.py`. |
| `standards-single-responsibility` | Each module owns exactly one concern. | Mission-file logic goes inside `doctrine.py`, which already owns the doctrine directory. Do not create `src/lore/doctrine_mission.py`. Do not fold `knight.py`'s code into `doctrine.py` — reproduce the resolver pattern, delete the module. |
| `technical-test-guidelines` | Unit tests never import `lore.cli`; every E2E file cites exactly one existing `conceptual-workflows-*` doc and is named for its slug; no locally defined `runner`/`project_dir` fixtures; no `US-N`/`SCENARIO-NNN` naming. | New unit tests import `lore.doctrine`, `lore.db`, `lore.health` directly. New E2E tests go into the existing anchored files wherever one exists; the one place no anchor exists is Conflict C-3. |

### Conflicts

| # | Rule id | The conflict | Options | Resolution |
|---|---------|-------------|---------|------------|
| C-1 | `doctrine-missions-prd` (internal), `conceptual-workflows-doctrine-show` | The PRD renders the doctrine-miss message as `Doctrine "tdd-feature-lite" not found` (double quotes) and in the same sentence instructs "the existing message and code for that case, **unchanged**". The live code at `cli.py:2055` emits `Doctrine 'tdd-feature-lite' not found` — single quotes. The two halves of the PRD sentence cannot both be satisfied. | **(a)** Ship the live single-quote text: honours "unchanged", keeps `tests/e2e/test_doctrine_show.py`'s current assertion green, and leaves the quoting style inconsistent with the new `Mission "x" not found in doctrine "y"` message the PRD also specifies. **(b)** Ship the PRD's literal double-quote text: the two `doctrine show` messages then match each other and the rest of the CLI's `"…"` convention, at the cost of a deliberate breaking change to an error string the PRD said was unchanged, and one rewritten E2E assertion. | **(a) — ship the live single-quote text.** `Doctrine 'x' not found`, byte-identical to `cli.py:2055`. The new mission-miss message keeps its double quotes. See Pre-Dev Notes. |
| C-2 | `conceptual-workflows-error-handling`, `decisions-017` | The PRD calls `lore doctrine edit` with no `-d`, `-m` or `--remove-mission` "a usage error" and specifies **exit 1** with a bare message. The error-handling contract assigns exit **2** to "an invalid flag, value or option combination… a command's own body raises others itself", and the sibling `lore edit` already raises `click.UsageError` (exit 2, `Error: ` prefix) for the identical "no field flags supplied" condition. | **(a)** Follow the PRD literally: `click.echo("Nothing to update: pass -d, -m, or --remove-mission", err=True); ctx.exit(1)`. Two commands then answer the same class of mistake with two different exit codes, and an orchestrator scripting against exit codes cannot tell "you passed nothing" from "the doctrine does not exist". **(b)** Raise `click.UsageError("Nothing to update: pass -d, -m, or --remove-mission")`: exit 2, stderr, `Error: ` prefix, consistent with `lore edit` and with the error-handling contract, at the cost of contradicting the PRD's stated exit code. **(c)** (b) plus an ADR pinning "a missing-required-combination error is exit 2" so the next feature does not re-decide it. | **(c) — `click.UsageError`, exit 2**, plus a new ADR pinning it. The PRD's exit 1 is overruled. See Pre-Dev Notes. |
| C-3 | `technical-test-guidelines` §3 | Every `tests/e2e/test_*.py` must cite exactly one existing `conceptual-workflows-*` document and be written **after** that document exists. `lore doctrine edit`'s new whole-file mode needs E2E coverage, and `conceptual-workflows-doctrine-edit` does not exist. The scribe, who writes codex documents, runs **after** the dev lanes. **Correction to the state of the tree:** §3 is not honoured by one outlier file — it is breached across most of `tests/e2e/`. All 23 `test_api_parity_*.py` files carry no `Spec:` anchor at all; neither do `tests/e2e/test_health.py`, `test_health_schemas*.py`, `test_codex_map.py`, `test_lore_init_schema_clean.py`, `test_schema_defaults_regression.py` or `test_init_*.py`. Several files anchor to transient ids that no longer exist in the codex (`test_doctrine_show.py` → `doctrine-design-file-us-004`, `test_doctrine_new.py` → `doctrine-design-file-us-007`, `test_doctrine_list.py` → `doctrine-design-file-us-001`, `test_knight_crud.py` → `group-param-us-002`, `test_filter_list.py` → `filter-list-subcommands-us-1`). Nothing validates the anchor, so `lore health` reports none of it. The files this feature rewrites (`test_doctrine_show.py`, `test_doctrine_new.py`, `test_doctrine_list.py`) get correct `conceptual-workflows-*` anchors in passing, because the E-table already names one per row. | **(a)** The surface-lane dev knight writes `conceptual-workflows-doctrine-edit` itself before its test, then `tests/e2e/test_doctrine_edit.py`: satisfies the rule literally, but a dev lane authors a codex document, which is the scribe's job and is in no lane's brief — and the scribe would then inherit a document it did not write, into a worklist row (`conceptual-workflows-doctrine-edit`, **create**) that already exists. **(b)** Coverage goes into the existing `tests/e2e/test_api_parity_doctrine.py`, whose stated subject is already `lore doctrine edit / delete`: no new document needed, and given the correction above it conforms to the suite's actual dominant practice rather than perpetuating a lone exception — at the cost of mixing parity assertions with functional ones in a file the spec already rewrites for E61. **(c)** The dev knight writes `tests/e2e/test_doctrine_edit.py` with no anchor and the scribe adds `conceptual-workflows-doctrine-edit` afterwards: the rule is violated for the length of the quest, `lore health --scope codex` cannot catch it, and the end state is identical to (a) one mission later. | **(c), amended — write the anchor anyway.** Surface lane writes `tests/e2e/test_doctrine_edit.py` anchored to `conceptual-workflows-doctrine-edit`; the scribe creates that document in the same quest. See Pre-Dev Notes. |
| C-4 | `decisions-010-public-api-stability`, `standards-public-api-stability` | **Eight** names leave `lore.api.__all__` (the five knight callables plus `Knight`, `Doctrine` and `DoctrineStep` — all three dataclasses are public today, `api.py:208-211`), **three** leave `lore.models.__all__`, two exported callables change their positional lists, four change keyword names, one exported dataclass field is renamed, and **nine exported callables change their return shape** (see the Breaking-change accounting block above). ADR-010's semver table makes every one of those "major bump **or** explicit `CHANGELOG.md` breaking-change notice". **Note the "or" does not reach the changelog entry itself**: ADR-010's Consequences ("Contributors update `CHANGELOG.md` and `lore.api.__all__` together whenever the public API changes") and `standards-public-api-stability`'s Rules for Contributors ("Every release that changes the public API must include a `CHANGELOG.md` entry") both impose a changelog entry unconditionally; the "or" only decides whether that entry is a `BREAKING CHANGE:` block under 0.12.0 or a major-version release note. `CHANGELOG.md` exists at the repository root; `pyproject.toml` reads `version = "0.11.0"`. The PRD's Success Criteria table targets "v0.12.0" and scopes no version bump and no changelog entry; nothing in this feature's requirements produces either. | **(a)** Ship 0.12.0 with an explicit `BREAKING CHANGE:` block in `CHANGELOG.md` naming every removed, renamed and reshaped symbol: satisfies the "or" branch of the table, keeps the pre-1.0 minor cadence, and relies on consumers reading the changelog. **(b)** Ship 1.0.0 (or 0.12.0 declared as the breaking release with major-bump semantics recorded): unambiguous for the `>=0.x,<1.0` pin `standards-public-api-stability` tells Realm to use, but commits the project to 1.0 semantics — "a deliberate decision, not a scheduled event" per that document's Transition to 1.0.0 section — earlier than planned. **(c)** Defer the release decision to a follow-up quest and land the code without a version or changelog change: leaves Realm able to take the breaking change on a minor bump with no notice, which is the exact outcome ADR-010 exists to prevent, and breaches the unconditional changelog obligation above regardless of which version number is eventually chosen. **In every option the changelog entry itself is owed.** Whichever is chosen, the obligation lands on a named unit — this feature currently has none, and the `CHANGELOG.md` row in Expected Codex Updates is the scribe's, who runs after the code lands and is not a release manager. | **(a) — `BREAKING:` bullets under `## [Unreleased]`, no version bump in this quest.** The **surface lane** owns the entry and `CHANGELOG.md` joins its file list. See Pre-Dev Notes. |
| C-5 | `standards-dry`, `conceptual-workflows-health` | The mission brief states "`--scope doctrines` audits **both frontmatter schemas**, id-vs-stem, duplicate ids, and … dangling `doctrine_mission` refs". Today all frontmatter schema validation runs in `_check_schemas` under `--scope schemas`, driven by the `_SCHEMA_KINDS` table, and `health_check` dedups only `dangling_codex_rite`. Putting schema validation into `_check_doctrines` as well would print every doctrine schema error twice on a bare `lore health`. **Both precedents exist in the tree and point opposite ways.** (i) `_check_glossary` calls `_glossary_schema_issues` *and* `_SCHEMA_KINDS` carries a `glossary` row, with no dedup — so a malformed `glossary.yaml` is already reported twice on a bare `lore health`, and `conceptual-workflows-health` records that as intentional: "The `glossary` schema kind also runs as part of `--scope schemas`" and "`--scope schemas` continues to validate every schema kind, including `glossary`, so a malformed glossary surfaces in `--scope schemas` even without `glossary` in the scope set." (ii) `dangling_codex_rite` runs under both `codex` and `rites` and **is** deduped by the one check-name gate in `health_check`. Today `_check_doctrines` performs no schema validation at all, so `doctrines` currently follows pattern (i)'s opposite — the `artifacts`/`watchers`/`codex` shape, where shape lives only in `--scope schemas`. | **(a)** Read "audits both frontmatter schemas" as "the `_SCHEMA_KINDS` table gains a `doctrine-mission-frontmatter` row and keeps the design row", leaving `--scope doctrines` to the cross-field checks JSON Schema cannot express — one home per rule, no duplicate rows, but `lore health --scope doctrines` alone reports no schema errors (which is already true today). **(b)** Run the two frontmatter validations inside `_check_doctrines` as well and extend the dedup set in `health_check` to cover them: `--scope doctrines` becomes self-contained as the brief's wording suggests, at the cost of two callers of one rule and a dedup mechanism that grows per check. **(c)** Move the two doctrine rows out of `_SCHEMA_KINDS` entirely and into `_check_doctrines`: self-contained and no duplication, but doctrine files stop being validated by `--scope schemas`, breaking the "schemas validates every entity file on disk" contract `conceptual-workflows-health` states. **What each choice costs, given the precedents:** (a) keeps `doctrines` where it is and is the no-code-change answer for the seam; (b) migrates `doctrines` onto the glossary pattern and therefore needs the dedup gate widened from one check name to a set, which is the one mechanism `health_check` has and the one `standards-dry` is being read against; (c) is the only option that removes a live capability (`--scope schemas` stops covering doctrine files) and is the only one that contradicts a codex document rather than a brief sentence. | **(a) — one home per rule.** `_SCHEMA_KINDS` gains a `doctrine-mission-frontmatter` row and keeps the design row; `_check_doctrines` does no schema validation. See Pre-Dev Notes. |

### New Decisions Needing an ADR

**Draft ADR 1 — A doctrine is prose for an orchestrator, not a graph Lore parses**

- **Context:** A doctrine has always carried a machine-readable step graph in
  `<name>.yaml` — step ids, `type`, `priority`, `knight`, `needs`, `notes` — and
  `doctrine.py` validated it, detected cycles in it and normalised it, while
  ADR-001 has always said Lore does not orchestrate. Nothing consumed the graph:
  `lore doctrine show` printed the YAML verbatim and an orchestrator read the
  prose to decide the order anyway. The validation machinery was a contract Lore
  enforced on a structure Lore never used.
- **Decision:** A doctrine carries no machine-readable workflow structure. Lore
  reads a doctrine's frontmatter for identity (`id`, `title`, `summary`) and a
  mission file's frontmatter for its index entry, and treats every body as an
  opaque string. Step ordering, step type, phases and dependencies are decided by
  the orchestrator from the design prose. `_validate_steps`, `_check_cycles`,
  `_normalize`, `load_doctrine`, `validate_doctrine_content`, `scaffold_doctrine`,
  `Doctrine`, `DoctrineStep` and the `doctrine-yaml` schema are deleted.
- **Alternatives rejected:** *Keep a reduced YAML with ordering only* — rejected:
  any parsed structure re-opens "what does Lore do with it", and an orchestrator
  that can read prose can read an ordered list in prose. *Move cycle detection
  into `lore health`* — rejected: there is no graph left to have a cycle in;
  relocating the code would preserve a concept the feature exists to delete.
  *Keep `Doctrine`/`DoctrineStep` in `models.__all__` as deprecated shells* —
  rejected: the PRD forbids compatibility shims, and a dataclass describing a
  structure that cannot be produced is a trap for Realm.

**Draft ADR 2 — The Knight entity is removed; a doctrine mission file is the only home for a reusable instruction**

- **Context:** A Knight held "how to be" and a doctrine step's `notes:` held
  "what to do", and every doctrine author decided afresh which of the two an
  instruction belonged in. The same guidance routinely landed in both. The
  `missions.knight` column, the `lore knight` command group, five public API
  names, a packaged schema, a health scope, a seeded tree and a `.gitignore`
  triad all existed to keep the second copy addressable.
- **Decision:** The Knight entity is removed outright — module, CLI group, the
  five API callables and the `Knight` type, `knight-frontmatter.yaml`,
  `lore health --scope knights`, `paths.knights_dir`, the `knight`
  `entity_location` row, the `knight` kind in `frontmatter_edit`, and
  `src/lore/defaults/knights/**`. Lore has six entity types. The one place a
  reusable instruction lives is the doctrine mission file that uses it, and a
  Lore mission reaches it through `missions.doctrine_mission`. No shim, no
  deprecation window, no alias.
- **Alternatives rejected:** *Deprecate `lore knight` for one release* —
  rejected: the PRD rules out a compatibility shim, and a deprecated command is
  a second addressable copy for exactly as long as it exists. *Keep Knight as a
  standalone reusable persona that a mission file can `include`* — rejected: an
  include is a template engine, which ADR-001 forbids, and it restores the
  two-places-to-put-an-instruction problem under a new name. *Auto-migrate
  authored knights into doctrine missions* — rejected by the PRD: Lore cannot
  know which doctrine a project's knight belongs to, and a wrong guess writes
  into a file the project owns.

**Draft ADR 3 — A multi-file entity write is staged in a temp directory and renamed into place**

- **Context:** No entity module has ever used `lore.safewrite`; `doctrine.py`,
  `knight.py`, `rite.py`, `artifact.py` and `watcher.py` all write with plain
  `Path.write_text()`, and today's two-file `create_doctrine` writes the YAML and
  then the design file with no atomicity between them. `safewrite` exists and is
  used by `init.py`, `manifest.py` and `reconcile.py`, and gives per-file
  atomicity plus a symlink and escape refusal. This feature's create path writes
  1 + N files at once and the PRD promises "no partial doctrine is ever left on
  disk".
- **Decision:** `doctrine.py` uses `lore.safewrite`. `create_doctrine` validates
  everything, builds the complete directory in a staging directory whose name
  begins with `.` inside `.lore/doctrines/`, writes each file through
  `safewrite.atomic_write_text`, and moves the whole tree into place with one
  `os.replace`. Doctrine discovery skips any path segment beginning with `.` or
  ending `.deleted`, which is what makes the staging directory invisible while it
  exists. `update_doctrine` validates everything first and then writes per file;
  its guarantee is scoped to validation failure and is stated as such.
- **Alternatives rejected:** *Sequential `write_text` as today* — rejected: it
  cannot deliver the PRD's stated guarantee for 1 + N files and would leave a
  half-built doctrine that `lore doctrine show` would then half-answer.
  *`tempfile.mkdtemp` outside `.lore/`* — rejected: `os.replace` across
  filesystems fails, and `/tmp` on the user's machine is routinely a different
  one. *Extend the swap to `update_doctrine`* — rejected: a rebuild would have to
  copy every `.md.deleted` tombstone to preserve it, for a crash window nobody
  has reported, and the PRD's own wording scopes the edit guarantee to validation
  failure.

**Draft ADR 4 — A seeded tree Lore stops shipping is removed by an explicit retired-tree list**

- **Context:** `lore init` re-seeds four `default/` trees and prunes each of what
  the release no longer ships, driven by `init.SEEDED_TREES`. The prune walks
  only trees still in that tuple. Removing the `knights` row — the obvious way to
  stop shipping knights — therefore stops anything from ever looking at
  `.lore/knights/default/` again, so every seeded knight stays on disk in every
  upgraded project, silently, forever. FR-24 requires the opposite.
  `conceptual-workflows-init-reconcile`'s reconciliation table cannot help: those
  paths are inside `.lore/` and the manifest records nothing inside `.lore/`.
- **Decision:** `init.py` carries a `RETIRED_SEED_TREES` tuple naming a seeded
  tree this release no longer ships. Every run walks each named tree, unlinks
  every regular file in it, reports each as
  `Removed <label>/<path> — no longer shipped`, and prunes the emptied
  directories, leaving any symlink and any unlinkable file alone exactly as
  `_prune_seeded_tree` does. `.lore/knights/default` is its first entry. Files a
  project authored outside the retired tree are not touched; they are collected
  and named once so the maintainer knows they are no longer read.
- **Alternatives rejected:** *Leave the `knights` row in `SEEDED_TREES` with an
  empty package* — rejected: `_defaults_tree_files` would have to walk a package
  that no longer exists, and the row would claim Lore still ships knights.
  *A one-off v0.12 cleanup in `run_init`* — rejected: this module was written
  specifically to avoid a per-version migration chain, and the second retired
  tree would have to repeat the trick. *Hard-code the removal in `reconcile.py`* —
  rejected: `reconcile` is about paths outside `.lore/` and its safety property
  is that a path in neither the desired nor the recorded set is never touched;
  a seeded tree is in neither set.

**Draft ADR 5 — An unresolvable doctrine mission is silent on read and reported only by `lore health`**

- **Context:** Today `lore show <mission-id>` prints
  `Warning: knight file "x" not found in .lore/knights/` on stdout when a
  mission's knight does not resolve, and `lore health --scope knights` reports
  the same condition as an error. A worker agent parsing `lore show` sees a
  warning line inside what it is reading as its brief, and the same fact is
  reported twice by two commands with two severities.
- **Decision:** A read never reports a broken reference. `lore show` on an
  unresolvable `doctrine_mission` prints the stored reference, omits the
  `--- Mission Instructions ---` section entirely, exits 0 and prints nothing
  else; `--json` carries the stored reference and `doctrine_mission_contents:
  null`. `lore health --scope doctrines` is the single reporter, at ERROR
  severity, for active missions only.
- **Alternatives rejected:** *Keep the warning line* — rejected: it puts
  diagnostic text inside a worker's instructions, where it is indistinguishable
  from content, and the PRD settles the behaviour as silent. *Exit non-zero on an
  unresolvable reference* — rejected: a worker would be unable to read a mission
  whose doctrine was edited under it, which is a read failing for an authoring
  mistake. *Report it on stderr instead of stdout* — rejected: it is still a
  second reporter of a fact `lore health` owns, and stderr noise on a successful
  read trains agents to ignore stderr.

