## Pre-Dev Notes

_Written by the orchestrator acting as the gate. The `spec-gate` human mission was
dropped at the user's request, with the user's explicit instruction to decide from
the purpose of the change rather than pause. Everything below is a ruling, not a
suggestion. A dev knight does not re-open any of it._

**The purpose these were ruled against.** One authoritative copy of every reusable
instruction. A doctrine is prose an orchestrator reads, not a graph Lore parses. No
second entity to keep in sync. Where a row was a genuine toss-up, the tie went to
whichever option removed no live capability and changed no contract this feature
did not have to change.

### Conflict rulings

**C-1 — option (a). Ship the live single-quote text.**
`Doctrine 'tdd-feature-lite' not found` stays byte-identical to `cli.py:2055`. The
PRD's double-quoted rendering was a transcription slip in a transient document, and
the same sentence's word "unchanged" is the half that carries the intent. An error
string is a contract a consumer may match on, and this feature has no reason to
change it — a contract enforced only by prior code and one E2E assertion is kept,
not quietly rewritten inside a story. `tests/e2e/test_doctrine_show.py`'s existing
assertion stays green and is not touched.

The new `Mission "recon" not found in doctrine "tdd-feature-lite"` keeps its double
quotes. The CLI's quoting is already mixed — the `Knight "x" not found in
.lore/knights/` message this one descends from is double-quoted — so the new string
follows its own nearest precedent. This leaves `doctrine show`'s two messages
quoting differently. That inconsistency is accepted deliberately: it is pre-existing,
it is cosmetic, and unifying it means breaking a live string for style. Do not
"fix" it in passing.

**C-2 — option (c). `click.UsageError`, exit 2, plus an ADR.**
The PRD's exit 1 is **overruled**. `conceptual-workflows-error-handling` is a settled
cross-cutting codex document; the PRD is a transient working one, and where they
disagree the codex wins. The sibling `lore edit` already raises `click.UsageError`
for the identical "no field flags supplied" condition at `cli.py:2316-2318`, so
exit 1 here would have two commands answering one class of mistake two ways.

Implement as `raise click.UsageError("Nothing to update: pass -d, -m, or --remove-mission")`
— exit 2, stderr, Click's `Error: ` prefix. Do not hand-roll it; ADR-017 forbids
rewording or re-coding a usage error.

Option (c) over (b) because the contract being followed here lives only in prior code
and a workflow document. That is exactly the case that earns an ADR rather than
another undocumented precedent. **Draft ADR 6 is approved on that basis** — see below.

**C-3 — option (c), amended. Write the anchor anyway.**
The surface lane writes `tests/e2e/test_doctrine_edit.py` and anchors it to
`conceptual-workflows-doctrine-edit`. The scribe creates that document later in this
same quest; the row is already in its worklist.

Rejected (a): a dev lane authoring a codex document crosses a lane boundary the
doctrine draws deliberately, and hands the scribe a document it did not write.
Rejected (b): mixing functional coverage into `test_api_parity_doctrine.py` bends the
file's stated subject to dodge a sequencing problem.

The amendment matters. Option (c) as written said "with no anchor"; that ships a file
permanently missing something the rule requires, because nothing later goes back to
add it. Writing the anchor now means the rule is satisfied at quest completion, which
is the unit that matters — the quest lands as one squash-merge, not as eight separate
states of the tree. The anchor naming a document that does not exist yet is invisible
to tooling either way: nothing validates it, which is the audit's own finding.

The suite-wide §3 anchor breach is **must-not-extend**, confirmed. Fix the anchors on
the three files this feature already rewrites, because they are open on the desk.
Repair nothing else.

**C-4 — option (a). `BREAKING:` bullets under `## [Unreleased]`. Surface lane owns it.**
`CHANGELOG.md` already carries a `## [Unreleased]` heading and an exact precedent for
this shape at line 211 — `BREAKING: removed from lore.api.__all__`, covering a prior
`find_knight` removal, written as a breaking-change notice in lieu of a major bump.
Follow that form.

Rejected (b): `standards-public-api-stability` calls the move to 1.0.0 "a deliberate
decision, not a scheduled event." This feature is not that decision and does not get
to make it by side effect. Rejected (c) outright: shipping a removal on a minor bump
with no notice is the precise outcome ADR-010 exists to prevent.

**No version bump in this quest.** `pyproject.toml` stays at `0.11.0`. The entry lands
under `[Unreleased]` and the human moves it under a version heading at release. A
feature branch does not bump a version.

**Owner: the surface lane, in its second commit.** `CHANGELOG.md` joins the surface
lane's file list — this is a gate decision, and it overrides the lane boundary as
written, because surface runs last among the code lanes and is the only one holding
both the `api.__all__` list and the core lane's `models.__all__` and field-rename
lists by the time it commits. Core lane: post your removals and renames to the surface
board so they reach the entry. Scribe: `CHANGELOG.md` is **not** yours; leave it alone.

The entry names every removed, renamed and reshaped symbol — eight from
`lore.api.__all__`, three from `lore.models.__all__`, the two changed positional lists,
the four renamed keywords, the renamed dataclass field, and all nine changed return
shapes.

**C-5 — option (a). One home per rule.**
`_SCHEMA_KINDS` gains a `doctrine-mission-frontmatter` row and keeps the existing
design row. `_check_doctrines` performs **no** frontmatter schema validation; it keeps
the cross-field checks JSON Schema cannot express — id-vs-stem, duplicate ids,
old-shape leftovers, dangling `doctrine_mission` refs.

The brief's phrase "`--scope doctrines` audits both frontmatter schemas" was mine and
it was imprecise. It is satisfied by both rows existing in the table. Rejected (b):
widening the dedup gate from one check name to a set adds a mechanism to `health.py`
to work around a duplication this option simply does not create. Rejected (c): it is
the only option that removes a live capability — `--scope schemas` would stop covering
doctrine files, contradicting `conceptual-workflows-health` rather than a brief
sentence.

This also leaves `doctrines` in the shape it already has, alongside
`artifacts`/`watchers`/`codex`. No seam moves.

### ADR rulings

**Draft ADRs 1 through 5: all APPROVED, as drafted.** Each records a genuinely new
load-bearing decision, and each one's alternatives-rejected section is sound.

- **ADR 1** — a doctrine is prose, not a graph. This is the change's spine.
- **ADR 2** — the Knight entity is removed outright; no shim, no deprecation window.
- **ADR 3** — a multi-file entity write stages in a dot-prefixed directory and renames
  into place. `doctrine.py` becomes the first entity module to use `lore.safewrite`,
  which is a precedent worth recording.
- **ADR 4** — `RETIRED_SEED_TREES`. Independently verified against source before
  approval: `_prune_seeded_tree` is only reachable from `_seed_tree`, which only runs
  for rows still in `SEEDED_TREES`, and `init.py:2312` additionally hardcodes
  `"knights"` in its package tuple. Deleting the row alone both orphans the files
  permanently and raises `KeyError`. FR-24 is unsatisfiable without this.
- **ADR 5** — an unresolvable reference is silent on read and reported only by
  `lore health`.

**Draft ADR 6 — NEW, approved: a missing-required-combination error is exit 2.**
Created by the C-2 ruling. Records that when a command requires at least one of a set
of flags and receives none, it raises `click.UsageError` — exit 2, stderr, Click's
standard prefix — rather than emitting its own message at exit 1. Existing behaviour on
`lore edit`, previously enforced by nothing but prior code. Scribe: write this one too.
`conceptual-workflows-error-handling` gains the conditional row the audit already
staged for it.

**No other ADR is written.** D27 through D30 are settled rules applied to lines the
spec had not reached, exactly as the audit classified them — no ADR is owed and none
should be invented. ADR-017 is untouched: removing a token from a `click.Choice` is not
an amendment trigger under its Constraint 3.

`decisions-006-id-references` is amended **in place** with a dated status-history line.
Its decision — agents address entities by ID through the CLI, never by file path — is
unchanged; only its enumeration of entity categories is stale. It is not superseded and
is not marked superseded.

### Standing rulings for every lane

**The four must-fix pre-existing breaches are in scope and are to be fixed**, because
this feature's own rewrite passes through each one. Leaving them is a new choice, not
inheritance: `_show_mission`'s CLI-only not-found warning (ADR-011), `doctrine_edit`'s
three whole-file errors on stdout, `doctrine show`'s local-`--json` error envelope on
stdout, and `update_doctrine`'s flat resolution — which, note, means `lore doctrine edit`
fails today on any `default/` doctrine, and after FR-27 every seeded doctrine is under
`default/`.

**The five must-not-extend breaches are not to be repaired.** Do not widen scope into
them. If one blocks a unit, block the mission and surface it.

**A decision conflict is never a dev knight's to resolve.** If a lane finds a row that
needs a ruling and does not have one here, `lore block` and surface it. Do not infer a
ruling from this document's reasoning and apply it to a new case.

**Where the committed tree and this spec disagree, the committed tree wins.** Record the
divergence on your board rather than rewriting an already-committed lane.

### Quality-gate ruling — added by the gate after the foundation lane

The foundation lane reported, and the gate independently verified, that
`uv run ruff check src/ tests/` and `uv run mypy src/` were **already failing on the
pre-quest tree at `9cce372`**, before any quest commit. The lane briefs say "gates
clean before each commit, never by suppression". Read literally against a dirty
baseline that instruction is unachievable, and an unachievable gate is an invitation
to the exact `# type: ignore` / `# noqa` suppression it was written to forbid. So it
is ruled, not left to each lane to reinterpret.

**Verified pre-quest baseline — `uv run mypy src/`: 183 errors in 17 files.**

| File | Errors | File | Errors |
|---|---|---|---|
| `cli.py` | 134 | `skills.py` | 2 |
| `db.py` | 21 | `rite.py` | 2 |
| `init.py` | 13 | `oracle.py` | 2 |
| `schemas/__init__.py` | 6 | `glossary.py` | 2 |
| `agents.py` | 6 | `frontmatter.py` | 2 |
| `health.py` | 4 | `watcher.py` | 1 |
| `doctrine.py` | 4 | `knight.py` | 1 |
| `frontmatter_edit.py` | 1 | `codex.py` | 1 |
| `artifact.py` | 1 | | |

**Verified pre-quest baseline — `uv run ruff check src/ tests/`: 3 errors**, all in
`tests/unit/test_lore_init.py` — `E402` ×2 at 425–426, `F401` unused `os` at 618.

**MYPY — the gate is "no new errors against the per-file baseline above", not zero.**
Repairing 183 pre-existing type errors is not this change's purpose; 134 of them sit in
`cli.py`, which would hand the surface lane a second feature's worth of work. The audit
listed none of them as a must-fix breach, and `standards`-driven type debt is a project
concern, not a doctrine-shape concern. Each lane records its files' counts before and
after and reports the delta. **A delta above zero on a file you touched is a defect —
fix it.** A file whose count is unchanged is fine, however high the count.

Never close the delta by suppression. A new `# type: ignore`, a broadened `Any`, a
loosened config setting or a deleted annotation is a failure of this gate, not a pass.

**RUFF — the gate stays a true zero, and the core lane clears it.**
Three trivial errors in one file is not debt, it is litter, and leaving it means the
two remaining lanes cannot tell their own lint failures from the tree's. **The core
lane fixes all three** as part of its work — it already owns `init.py` and therefore
`tests/unit/test_lore_init.py`. Remove the unused import; move or properly justify the
two late imports. After the core lane commits, `uv run ruff check src/ tests/` reports
zero, and the surface lane and the scribe are held to zero.

This is the only scope the gate adds beyond the spec, it is three lines, and it buys a
hard gate for the rest of the quest.

### Two further gate rulings — added after the core lane

**S7's `grep -rn knight src/lore/ tests/` gate is redefined. It is not zero.**
The core lane reported it unsatisfiable as written and the gate verified the tree.
Two categories of match are **legitimate and permanent — do not remove them**:

- `src/lore/migrations/v3_to_v4.py`, `v4_to_v5.py`, `v6_to_v7.py` (14 matches). A
  migration is a historical record of DDL that ran. `missions.knight` existed; the
  migration chain must keep naming it. Editing these to satisfy a grep would falsify
  the schema history and break the upgrade path off an older database.
- `src/lore/init.py` lines 403, 406, 414–415 — the `RETIRED_SEED_TREES` entry for
  `knights/default` and its field docstrings. This is the mechanism that *removes*
  seeded knights; it cannot do that without naming them. Approved as ADR 4.
- The corresponding migration tests under `tests/` for the same reason.

**Everything else is zero.** After S7, the only matches outside those two categories
are a defect. Surface lane: that means `api.py`, `cli.py`, `prompts.py` (lines 37 and
41 — the `ACCESS_SCOPE_NOTE` entity list and the "five skills" docstring), **and
`src/lore/scoped.py` lines 55 and 79**, two knight docstring sentences that sit in no
lane's file list. `scoped.py` joins the surface lane for those two lines only — a
docstring-only edit, the same call the foundation lane made on its own files, and the
alternative is a knight reference surviving in the tree because no unit happened to
own it.

**The full suite is NOT green at the end of the surface lane, and that is expected.**
`tests/unit/test_health_write_report.py::test_write_report_true_report_file_contains_markdown_table`
asserts `"No issues found."` on a fresh `lore init`. Verified: a fresh init still seeds
old-shape doctrines, `--scope doctrines` now warns on them, and the assertion fails.
It closes when the scribe lands FR-27 and the seeded doctrines convert. This is a real
ordering consequence of `src/lore/defaults/` belonging to the scribe rather than to a
dev lane, not a defect and not something the surface lane may fix by weakening the
assertion.

- **Surface lane:** finish with the suite green *except* for tests failing solely on
  this dependency. Name every one of them explicitly, by full test id, on the scribe's
  board. A test you cannot name and justify that way is a failure you own.
- **Scribe:** those named tests are part of your done-criteria. `uv run pytest` is
  fully green before you mark done — no exceptions inherited, no exceptions added. If
  one of them still fails after FR-27, it is a defect and you block rather than
  documenting it.
